      whatItSays: "Moment of inertia I is the rotational equivalent of mass — it measures resistance to angular acceleration. Unlike mass, it depends not just on how much matter there is, but on how that matter is distributed relative to the rotation axis. Mass farther from the axis contributes more, weighted by r².",
      example: "A figure skater with arms outstretched has a large moment of inertia and spins slowly. Pulling arms in reduces I, and since angular momentum L = Iω is conserved, the angular velocity ω must increase — she spins faster. This is conservation of angular momentum made visible.",
      deepMeaning: "The general definition is I = ∫r² dm — a mass integral weighted by the square of the distance from the axis. The factor of 2/5 for a solid sphere versus 2/3 for a hollow sphere shows that a hollow sphere has more of its mass concentrated at large r, giving it a larger moment of inertia and making it harder to spin up. When solid and hollow spheres race down a ramp, the solid sphere (smaller I/mr²) wins because less energy goes into rotation and more into translation.",
      derivation: "Starting from rotational kinetic energy KE = ½Iω² and requiring it equal the sum ½Σmᵢvᵢ² = ½Σmᵢ(ωrᵢ)² for all particles: KE = ½ω²Σmᵢrᵢ². Defining I = Σmᵢrᵢ² gives KE = ½Iω², perfectly analogous to ½mv² for translation.",
      whoDiscovered: "Christiaan Huygens calculated moments of inertia for simple bodies in the 1650s–1670s. Leonhard Euler systematized the theory of rigid body rotation in the 18th century.",
      whyItMatters: "Every rotating object in engineering — flywheels, turbine blades, vehicle wheels, gyroscopes — is designed with its moment of inertia as a key parameter. It determines how quickly motors can spin up machinery and how stably gyroscopes maintain orientation.",
      integralForm: "\\begin{aligned} I_{\\text{point}} &= mr^2 \\\\ I_{\\text{solid sphere}} &= \\tfrac{2}{5}mr^2 \\\\ I_{\\text{hollow sphere}} &= \\tfrac{2}{3}mr^2 \\\\ I_{\\text{solid disk}} &= \\tfrac{1}{2}mr^2 \\\\ I_{\\text{rod (centre)}} &= \\tfrac{1}{12}mL^2 \\\\ I_{\\text{rod (end)}} &= \\tfrac{1}{3}mL^2 \\end{aligned}",
