      deepMeaning: "Specific heat capacity c is a material property that measures how much energy per unit mass is needed to raise temperature by 1 K. Water has an exceptionally high specific heat (c = 4186 J/kg·K), which is why oceans moderate coastal climates and why water is used as coolant in engines. At the microscopic level, c reflects how many degrees of freedom the molecules have to absorb energy. A monatomic ideal gas has only 3 translational degrees of freedom, giving a low c. Complex polyatomic molecules have additional rotational and vibrational modes, giving a higher c. This is the equipartition theorem in action. For gases, c_p (at constant pressure) is always greater than c_v (at constant volume) because at constant pressure the gas must also do work expanding against its surroundings.",
      derivation: "",
      whoDiscovered: "Joseph Black developed the concept of specific heat experimentally in the 1750s–1760s, distinguishing heat from temperature for the first time.",
      whyItMatters: "Every thermal engineering calculation — heating, cooling, engine cycles, climate modelling — uses this equation as the starting point.",
