      example: "For nitrogen at 300 K: v_p = √(2 × 8.314 × 300 / 0.028) ≈ 422 m/s. The three speeds satisfy v_p < v_mean < v_rms, with ratio approximately 1 : 1.128 : 1.225.",
      deepMeaning: "The three characteristic speeds of the Maxwell-Boltzmann distribution obey: v_p : v_mean : v_rms = √2 : √(8/π) : √3 ≈ 1 : 1.128 : 1.225. The distribution is skewed to the right — there is a long tail of fast molecules. This high-speed tail is crucial for chemistry: only molecules with enough energy to overcome the activation barrier can react, and even a small temperature increase dramatically increases the fraction of molecules in the high-speed tail, explaining the Arrhenius equation for reaction rates. The tail also governs atmospheric escape — the fastest molecules in a planet's upper atmosphere can exceed escape velocity and be permanently lost to space, which is why the Moon has no atmosphere.",
      derivation: "Set df(v)/dv = 0 where f(v) is the Maxwell-Boltzmann distribution. Differentiating and setting to zero gives the condition 2v - mv³/k_BT = 0, which yields v_p = √(2k_BT/m).",
      whoDiscovered: "James Clerk Maxwell, 1860. The full Maxwell-Boltzmann distribution was extended by Ludwig Boltzmann to include potential energy contributions.",
